step D: evaluating
triage: fix_tests: Other scenarios do get a 'withdraw' option, such as the battle 2 test where seat 1 withdraws, so the game does offer it in normal play. Only this battle_over scenario fails, with neither seat ever offered 'withdraw', which is what the rules require while battle_over is true: the remaining rounds pass with no actions at all.
rewriting the independent tests
step D: evaluating
triage: revise_rules: The game is built correctly, yet a thoughtless policy (always the first option) wins every decided match from either seat and always taking the last option loses every match. Play is decided by one mechanical habit, not by decisions: deploying face-up beats improvising for almost every card, and withdrawing only gives points away.
